  "problem": "",
  "worked_solution": "Let's denote by $x$ the unknown length of the third side.\n\nAccording to the triangle inequality theorem, the length of any side must be\n\n- greater than the difference between the lengths of the other two sides, and\n\n- less than the sum of those lengths.\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Difference} & <x<\\text{Sum} \\\\ 7-5 & <x<7+5 \\\\ 2 & <x<12.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nThe above inequalities are strict. So, $x$ can't be equal to $2$ or $12.$ Therefore, the smallest possible **integer** value is $3$ while the largest is $11.$",
